---
title: Norwegian Wood
subject: Haruki Murakami
genre: Books
rating: 4
date: 2026-08-14
excerpt: >-
  Grief rendered so plainly it stops being sad and starts being weather. Toru
  drifts; so do we.
---

Murakami without the talking cats turns out to be Murakami at his most exposed. There are no parallel worlds here, no wells to climb down — only a narrator in his late thirties hearing a Beatles song on a plane and losing an afternoon to 1969. The fantastical machinery is gone, and what remains is a very ordinary young man surrounded by people who are quietly deciding whether to stay alive.

Toru is passive in a way that irritates some readers and seems, to me, entirely accurate. He does not rescue Naoko because he cannot; he does not choose Midori because choosing would mean admitting the other thing has ended. The novel is honest about how little agency grief leaves a nineteen-year-old.

> Death is not the opposite of life, but a part of it.

Where it falters is in its women, who occasionally speak in the register of a thesis rather than a person, and in a middle section at the sanatorium that goes slack. But the writing about depression is unusually free of metaphor — it is described as a physical fact, like altitude, and that plainness is what makes it land.

The last page is a phone booth and a question the narrator cannot answer. Twenty years on, that remains the correct ending: not resolution, just the recognition that some grief simply becomes the climate you live in.
